**EXERCISE 1.21.** Work out in greater detail the proof of 1.10. Remark that the neighbourhood system over $|\mathcal{D}|$ so constructed is positive, thereby obtaining in a different way the same kind of conclusion as in 1.20. Show also that the system over $|\mathcal{D}|$ is complete in the sense that every filter is fixed by a unique member of the underlying set. (A filter is *fixed by a point* iff it is the filter of *all* neighbourhoods containing that point.) Remark that a complete system is one where tokens and (partial) elements can always be identified (under a suitable one-one correspondence). Show also that consistency of a set $\{ X_i \mid i < n \}$ of neighbourhoods in $\mathcal{D}$ is equivalent to saying

$$
\bigcap_{i < n} [ X_i ] \neq \emptyset.
$$

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`IsComplete`).** A neighbourhood system is complete when every ideal element is the filter of all neighbourhoods containing a unique token.

**Theorem (`bracket_nonempty_iff`).** The bracket $[X]$ is nonempty if and only if $X$ is a neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Any element of $[X]$ forces $X$ to be a member; conversely the principal of $X$ lies in $[X]$. ∎

**Theorem (`consistent_iff_iInter_bracket_nonempty`).** A finite sequence of neighbourhoods is consistent if and only if the intersection of their brackets is nonempty.

*Proof.* Consistency is equivalent to membership of the finite intersection, hence to nonempty bracket of that intersection, which unwraps as a common point of the individual brackets. ∎

**Theorem (`tokenSystem_complete`).** The token system over $|\mathcal{D}|$ is complete.

*Proof.* Each ideal element of the token system is fixed by the unique underlying element of $|\mathcal{D}|$ recovered by `ofToken`. ∎

**Theorem (`tokenSystem_isPositive`).** The token system is positive.

*Proof.* Intersection of brackets is the bracket of the intersection. ∎

**Theorem (`tokenSystem_toToken_bijective`).** Tokens of the token system biject with ideal elements of $\mathcal{D}$.

*Proof.* The domain isomorphism of Theorem 1.10 is bijective on underlying sets. ∎

**Theorem (`exercise_1_21`).** Consistency is characterised by nonempty bracket intersections; the token system is complete and positive; and tokens identify with elements.

*Proof.* Assemble the consistency equivalences with completeness, positivity, and bijectivity of `toToken`. ∎
